Known limitations
=================

At high latitudes a moonrise that only grazes the effective horizon — a
near-tangent crossing where the Moon clears it by mere arcminutes — can be
missed: the iterated altitude correction in moonrise() oscillates around
such a double root instead of settling, and the non-converged probe is now
rejected rather than kept (Tromsø, 2026 Sep 29 is a concrete case; the
Moon dips below the horizon for ~35 minutes and returns). The affected
lunar day then silently merges with its neighbor. Recovering these events
would take a bisection fallback on the altitude function when the Newton
step fails to converge.

Formerly documented here: two entries about moonrise inaccuracy near new
moon (Kolkata, April 2026) and a spurious extra moonrise near grazing
horizon crossings (Moscow, August 2026). Both turned out to be the same
root cause: moonrise()'s three-point Lagrange interpolation of the Moon's
right ascension did not unwrap across the 0h/24h (2π) boundary, so a
crossing within the 3-day window corrupted the interpolated position and
threw off the Newton correction in the convergence loop — in the Kolkata
case by tens of minutes, in the Moscow case badly enough to fabricate a
bogus rise event. Fixed by unwrapping the right-ascension samples before
building the interpolator (later superseded: the correction loop now
evaluates the true ephemeris at each iterate, so no interpolation — and
no unwrapping — is involved at all). The divergence guard that had been
added as a workaround for the Moscow symptom (rejecting corrections >3h
from the ApproxTimes seed) is no longer needed and has been removed;
non-convergence after 10 rounds is instead reported as an error, which
also stopped fabricated rises on high-latitude days where the Moon never
truly crossed the horizon (Tromsø, May/June/August/December 2026).
